TI  - The influence of environmental factors and inducible enzyme fermentation broth on the lethality of Beauveria bassiana to Xylotrechus rusticus larvae.
PG  - 108702
LID - S0022-2011(26)00178-3 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.jip.2026.108702 [doi]
AB  - Entomopathogenic fungi are promising eco-friendly biocontrol agents due to their insect-parasitizing properties, and among them, Beauveria bassiana (B. bassiana) is widely used as a broad-spectrum biopesticide. This study aimed to investigate the effects of environmental factors on the pathogenicity of a B. bassiana strain (tentatively designated Bb01), which was isolated from larvae of Xylotrechus rusticus (X. rusticus). Specifically, Bb01 achieved the highest lethality against X. rusticus larvae among the tested conditions at 25 C and 95% relative humidity. Furthermore, ultraviolet (UV) irradiation was found to affect the lethality of the Bb01 strain. After 10 min of irradiation, the Bb01 strain still retained strong lethality, reaching 73.33%. Beyond environmental factors, the inducible enzyme fermentation broths of chitinase, lipase, and protease all exerted synergistic effects on the lethality of Bb01 against the larvae. In treatment groups supplemented with these three inducible enzyme fermentation broths, the cumulative mortality of X. rusticus larvae reached 100% at 10 days post-treatment. Among these groups, the one amended with lipase fermentation broth exhibited the shortest median lethal time (LT(50)) of 2.36 days.
